pansion carries `:yin/source-node` instead — a declared ref to a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:702:carries neither. Only `:yin/source-node` is a declared ref. The names:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:703:`:yin/source-call` keeps its existing name in this narrowed value role so\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:704:that existing log data is not invalidated by the split; `:yin/source-node`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:711:`yin.vm.macro/event-schema` declares these attributes (`:yin/source-node`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:712:`:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root` as refs; `:yin/source-call` deliberately\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:727:- `:yin/source-call` on an event is a value, never a ref: the call's eid\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:731:  (`dao.space.index.as-observer.md` §3.2). `:yin/source-node` is the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:926:  `:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability`, `:yin/source-call`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:1171:| 10 | Provenance identity: same tempid in two batches, generated syntax, program↔log | §4.1 `:yin/source-batch`; nested `source-node` into the log copy;                |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:309:   :yin/source-call {:db/valueType :db.type/ref}, ; EID of the\n   640\t\n   641\t### 3.5 Guards\n   642\t\n   643\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   644\t| Guard                                 | Scope                              | Default   | Error                                                                         |\n   645\t+=======================================+====================================+===========+===============================================================================+\n   646\t| depth                                 | per expansion chain                | 100       | `{:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}`                                          |\n   647\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   648\t| nodes per expansion                   | one macro's output                 | 10,000    | `{:kind :datom-guard :scope :expansion :emitted :call}`                       |\n   649\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   650\t| datoms per batch                      | cumulative over one `expand-batch` | unlimited | `{:kind :datom-guard :scope :batch :emitted}`                                 |\n   651\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   652\t| steps                                 | one body invocation                | 100,000   | `{:kind :fuel-guard :steps}`                                                  |\n   653\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   654\t| cyclic / dangling / host-valued input | admission                          | —         | `{:kind :malformed-input :reason :cyclic\\|:dangling-ref\\|:host-value :eid}`   |\n   655\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   656\t| arity / output / suspended            | —                                  | —         | `{:kind :arity}` `{:kind :invalid-output :at}` `{:kind :suspended}`           |\n   657\t+---------------------------------------+------------------------------------+-----------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   658\t\n   659\tGuard failures are `:error` results (decision 11); `:malformed-input` is\n   660\ttoo, so a corrupt batch is logged and skipped rather than re-read forever.\n   661\t\n   662\t---\n   663\t\n   664\t## 4. Provenance\n   665\t\n   666\t### 4.1 The expansion event\n   667\t\n   668\tEvery attempt, successful or not, produces one event; so does every batch\n   669\trejected at admission. Events and the `m`-tagged output are appended to the\n   670\t**log** medium, when the expander has one; `program-out` receives only\n   671\tprogram.\n   672\t\n   673\t```\n   674\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\n   675\t[ev :yin/source-batch   <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; which source batch; always present\n   676\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; VALUE, undeclared: the call's eid as it appeared in the source batch, qualified by :yin/source-batch; only on an initial (non-nested) expansion\n   677\t[ev :yin/source-node    <node-eid>           t default-op]   ; DECLARED REF, log-local: the call node in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output; only on a nested expansion\n   678\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda or an admission failure\n   679\t[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)\n   680\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success; names the LOG copy\n   681\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure; plain data naming entities, never carrying input values\n   682\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; logical, never a host clock\n   683\t```\n   684\t\n   685\tAn **admission-failure event** is the same entity with only `:yin/type`,\n   686\t`:yin/source-batch`, `:yin/error`, and `:yin/timestamp`: the batch had no\n   687\tusable call or macro to name. It is allocated from `:alloc`'s watermark\n   688\t(which needs no index), so a corrupt batch still produces exactly one\n   689\tplain-data event, advances `:ctx` once, and is retried through the same\n   690\tper-medium staging as any other log payload — never regenerated.\n   691\t\n   692\tThe log copy of expansion output carries `m = ev`; log copies of unchanged\n   693\toperands and ancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro\n   694\toutput). **`program-out` datoms all carry `default-op`**: `m = ev` would be\n   695\ta cross-medium ref (§4.2). An initial expansion's `:yin/source-call` is a\n   696\tvalue, not a ref: the call's eid as it appeared in the source batch,\n   697\tqualified by `:yin/source-batch`, which no index or transactor resolves. A\n   698\tnested expansion carries `:yin/source-node` instead — a declared ref to a\n   699\tnode in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output — which is how the\n   700\tchain source → event → root → event → root is walked for generated syntax.\n   701\tA successful expansion carries exactly one of the two; an admission failure\n   702\tcarries neither. Only `:yin/source-node` is a declared ref. The names:\n   703\t`:yin/source-call` keeps its existing name in this narrowed value role so\n   704\tthat existing log data is not invalidated by the split; `:yin/source-node`\n   705\tis new and is named for what it points at — a node of the log copy, the\n   706\tterm this document already uses for AST nodes there — because \"call\" would\n   707\tmislead: a nested expansion names the outer expansion's output node, not a\n   708\tcall site in any source batch.\n   709\t\"Every datom this expansion produced\" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]` on the log.\n   710\t\n   711\t`yin.vm.macro/event-schema` declares these attributes (`:yin/source-node`,\n   712\t`:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root` as refs; `:yin/source-call` deliberately\n   713\tundeclared, so its value is left alone) for compositions that commit a\n   714\tlog to `dao.space`; the transactor relocates only declared refs, so a\n   715\tcomposition merges this fragment with `yin.vm/schema` before committing.\n   716\t\n   717\t### 4.2 Identity across media\n   718\t\n   719\t`program-in`, `program-out`, and the log are different media. A ref written\n   720\ton one that names an entity on another is meaningful only when the named\n   721\tid is **durable** — a permanent positive id from a committed stream. The\n   722\ttransactor (`dao.space.transact`) resolves tempids within one transaction;\n   723\tcommitting two batches separately does not connect them. Therefore:\n   724\t\n   725\t- `program-out` batches are self-contained: unchanged subtrees are copied,\n   726\t  with fresh ids, not referenced (decision 8).\n   727\t- `:yin/source-call` on an event is a value, never a ref: the call's eid\n   728\t  *as it appeared in the source batch*, qualified by `:yin/source-batch`.\n   729\t  It is descriptive until both media are committed under one resolution,\n   730\t  and the join that would make it a ref is the composition's\n   731\t  (`dao.space.index.as-observer.md` §3.2). `:yin/source-node` is the\n   732\t  log-local counterpart: a declared ref, resolved within the log batch it\n   733\t  appears in and never across media. `:yin/macro` is written only when the\n   734\t  macro's id is durable; otherwise `:yin/macro-name` carries the name.\n   735\t  Names are metadata, never identity repair.\n   736\t- `:yin/expansion-root` names the **log copy** of the output. The executed\n   737\t  program on `program-out` is a distinct copy with distinct ids; this design\n   738\t  promises no id-level correspondence between them. A composition that needs\n   739\t  one commits program and log in one transaction (they are one batch to the\n   740\t  transactor) or records the mapping itself.\n   741\t- A pipeline that wants queryable provenance commits `program-in` first and\n   742\t  expands against durable ids — the ordering rule of\n   743\t  `cross-language-macro.md` §4, retained. `ADR 0003` makes `dao.space` the\n   744\t  medium for that commit.\n   745\t\n   746\t### 4.3 Authority\n   747\t\n   748\tWrite authority on a medium is the whole model. Defining a macro is\n   749\tappending a definition to `program-in`; running a program is appending to\n   750\t`program-out`; both are decided by whoever holds the writer, and\n   751\t`dao.stream.md` puts that decision in the composition. A Shibi\n   752\tcapability, when it exists, is verified at the medium's boundary before the\n      [op [] eid])))\n\n\n(defn- collect-tempids\n  \"Collect all tempids from parsed ops and extra datoms.\"\n  [ops effective-ref-attrs extra-datoms]\n  (let [op-tempids (mapcat (fn [{:keys [e a v m-raw]}]\n                             (cond-> []\n                               (tempid? e) (conj e)\n                               (and (contains? effective-ref-attrs a)\n                                    (tempid? v))\n                               (conj v)\n                               (tempid? m-raw) (conj m-raw)))\n                           ops)\n        extra-tempids (mapcat (fn [d]\n                                (cond-> []\n                                  (tempid? (nth d 0)) (conj (nth d 0))\n                                  (and (contains? effective-ref-attrs (nth d 1))\n                                       (tempid? (nth d 2)))\n                                  (conj (nth d 2))\n                                  (tempid? (nth d 4)) (conj (nth d 4))))\n                              extra-datoms)]\n    (into #{} (concat op-tempids extra-tempids))))\n\n\n(defn- resolve-tempids\n  \"Assign sequential permanent entity IDs to tempids.\"\n  [tempids start-eid]\n  (let [sorted-tempids (sort (fn [a b]\n                               (cond (and (number? a) (number? b)) (compare a b)\n                                     (and (string? a) (string? b)) (compare a b)\n                                     (number? a) -1\n                                     :else 1))\n                             tempids)]\n    (zipmap sorted-tempids\n            (range start-eid (+ start-eid (count sorted-tempids))))))\n\n\n(defn- apply-tempid-map\n  \"Replace tempids with permanent entity IDs in parsed ops.\"\n  [ops tempid-map effective-ref-attrs]\n  (mapv (fn [op]\n          (let [e' (if (tempid? (:e op))\n                     (get tempid-map (:e op) (:e op))\n                     (:e op))\n                v' (if (and (contains? effective-ref-attrs (:a op))\n                            (tempid? (:v op)))\n                     (get tempid-map (:v op) (:v op))\n                     (:v op))\n                m-raw' (if (tempid? (:m-raw op))\n                         (get tempid-map (:m-raw op) (:m-raw op))\n                         (:m-raw op))]\n            (assoc op\n                   :e e'\n                   :v v'\n                   :m-raw m-raw')))\n        ops))\n\n10:and a supplied ref schema, and nothing below knows or cares what the rows\n59:   supplied ref schema, nothing else: it never evaluates, never inspects an\n69:All of `dao.space` is on `dao.stream` (no v1 references remain). Its\n109:   carries per-batch negative tempids that *repeat*. `dao.space.index` never\n130:               :schema   {attr {:db/valueType :db.type/ref ...}} ; supplied; which attrs are refs\n205:   by `local-datom?` already, so a negative ref-valued `v` needs no schema\n219:   slots — `e`, a `v` under an attribute `:schema` declares a ref, and `m`,\n220:   the metadata *reference* — and the transactor already resolves all three\n221:   (`dao.space.transact` collects `m` tempids explicitly). The index does the\n223:   violation is a whole-batch defect. In `:unresolved` mode every tempid in\n224:   the batch — as `e`, as a declared-ref `v`, as `m`, or only as one of the\n225:   latter two (a reference to an entity the batch never describes) — is\n228:   row `e`, then declared-ref `v`, then `m` — so allocation is identical on\n267:the same `[e a v]` — the same *resolved* `e`; a reused negative tempid in a\n285:tempids allocated *per batch* (from `(- datom/first-user-id)` downward, by\n289:`dao.space.transact` resolves tempids within one transaction; the observer\n297:observer minting positive ids for tempids cannot share an index without\n305:tempids and owns every positive user id in its index, allocating from\n308:**The mode matrix.** Identity-bearing slots are `e`, a declared-ref `v`,\n309:and `m`. Ids partition into three disjoint classes: **tempid** `id < 0`;\n315:| `e` | reserved or user-positive pass through — exactly what `local-datom?` and the transactor admit today, so Phase 0′ parity is unconditional; tempid → defect | tempid → allocated; reserved or user-positive → defect (an unexplained positive reference: the writer cannot know the observer's ids) |\n316:| declared-ref `v` | reserved or user-positive pass; tempid → defect (this is the schema-aware check `local-datom?` alone does not make) | tempid → allocated; reserved passes; user-positive → defect |\n317:| `m` | reserved or user-positive pass; tempid → defect | reserved passes; tempid → allocated; user-positive → defect |\n318:| undeclared `v` | any value, untouched | any value, untouched |\n333:it there) and therefore a contract on compositions: joins across sources go\n340:### 3.2 Resolution facts\n342:For every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n* —\n347:[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n367:`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n368:today — and a declared ref *always* resolves within the batch it appears\n373:a **value**, qualified by the batch it refers to, under an attribute the\n374:schema does not declare a ref; the join is then value-based over\n376::dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — and is the composition's, since only the\n386:batch) and a log-local ref (nested expansion: a node in the log copy). One\n387:attribute cannot be both. It must split — an undeclared value attribute for\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
